#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193860 :tutorial/content "We can always represent the solution of an inequality using a number line.\n\nFor example, let's express the solution of the following inequality using a number line:\n\n$$\n5x \\geq 30\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $5.$ To isolate $x,$ we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $5.$ So, we divide both sides of the inequality by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & \\ge 30 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & \\ge \\frac{30}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $5$ from the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{5} & \\ge 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & \\ge 6 \\\\ x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\geq 6.$ This solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3a3d5a89d9181af5de9658ad2ac00cd8026a3050d48765dba5f5b23d3a0e7bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c86ff43d-11e0-54fc-bafd-5638148ec372" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6759 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
